# hwlog

> A custom hardware log file type that only adds new ENTRY blocks and never rewrites old ones.

## Purpose
Explain the .hwlog format so a non-programmer can see it is an append-only notebook for prototypes. Source: fitzyracing1/hwlog.

## How it works (plain English)
1. Open a .hwlog file.
2. Add a new ENTRY block at the end.
3. Leave every older block untouched.
4. A checker can confirm the blocks are well formed.

## Key ideas
- Append-only (new lines go at the end)
- ENTRY block (one stamped note about the hardware)
- Custom file type (a named format, not a secret language)

## The actual code
```
# source: fitzyracing1/hwlog
# format idea
ENTRY
  when: <stamp>
  part: <name>
  note: <what changed>
END
```

## Line-by-line translation
1. ENTRY → “start one hardware note”
2. when → “when this note was written”
3. part → “which piece of hardware”
4. note → “what happened to it”
5. END → “close the block so the next note can start”

## Decisions the program makes
- If a write would change an old block → refuse and append instead
- If a block is missing END → the file is not valid

## What a person would notice
The log grows. Old notes stay put.

## Current status
Barrage clone of the custom file-type repo.

## Next possible steps
Add one ENTRY in a real .hwlog file in the source repo.
